# Biomedical Equipment Error Log Triage

## Run the CLI in Docker (PowerShell)

### 1. Start Docker Desktop

Open Docker Desktop and wait until its engine is running.

### 2. Open PowerShell in the project folder

In File Explorer, open the `Project` folder, click the address bar, type
`powershell`, and press Enter. This opens PowerShell in that folder without
needing to use a specific computer's path. Alternatively, use `Set-Location`
with the path to the `Project` folder on your own computer:

```powershell
Set-Location "{path\to\your}\Project"
```

The current folder should contain `Dockerfile`, `requirements.txt`, and
`main.py`. The commands below assume PowerShell and that folder.

### 3. Prepare the `.env` file

Create or edit `.env` in the project folder. Add your own API credentials and
settings, or request the team's `.env` file from the Group 2 leader through a
private channel. Do not post credentials in the README, source code, or a
public chat, and do not commit `.env`.

```dotenv
AI_API_KEY=your_api_key
AI_BASE_URL=https://api.groq.com/openai/v1
AI_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-20b
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=
```

`AI_BASE_URL` and `AI_MODEL` should match the AI provider and model for your
key. Telegram settings are optional. The Docker image does not contain `.env`;
`docker run --env-file .env` passes the values into the container at runtime.

### 4. Create persistent-data and log folders

```powershell
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force .\data, .\logs
```

`data` stores processed records on your computer. Put optional machine log
files to ingest in `logs`. The app's own diagnostic file, `app.log`, is also
written there when running in Docker.

### 5. Build the Docker image

```powershell
docker build -t inf1103-group2-errorlogging .
```

This builds an image named `inf1103-group2-errorlogging` using the `Dockerfile` in the
current folder. Rebuild it after changing application code or the Dockerfile.

### 6. Run the interactive app with mounted folders

```powershell
docker run --rm -it `
  --mount "type=bind,source=$((Get-Location).Path)\data,target=/data" `
  --mount "type=bind,source=$((Get-Location).Path)\logs,target=/app/logs" `
  --env-file .env `
  -e DATA_FILE=/data/processed_records.json `
  inf1103-group2-errorlogging
```

The `data` mount maps `Project\data` on your computer to `/data` in the
container. Records are saved to `Project\data\processed_records.json` and
remain there after the container exits. The `DATA_FILE` setting tells
`data_manager.py` to use that mounted location.

The `logs` mount maps `Project\logs` to `/app/logs`. The app writes
`app.log` there; you can also put machine log files in this folder and select
them for ingestion by entering a path such as `/app/logs/errors.txt` when
prompted. This mount is writable so the app can create its diagnostic log.
If you do not need file ingestion, you can leave `logs` empty; the app still
runs normally.

`--rm` removes the container when it exits, while the mounted data and log
files stay on your computer. Select **Exit** in the app menu to stop it.

### 7. Run the offline logic tests in Docker

After building the image, run:

```powershell
docker run --rm inf1103-group2-errorlogging python test_logic.py
```

The tests mock AI and Telegram calls, so they do not require credentials or
send real requests. To rebuild before testing after code changes:

```powershell
docker build -t inf1103-group2-errorlogging .
docker run --rm inf1103-group2-errorlogging python test_logic.py
```


